+# The familiar puzzle
 
 One twin stays on Earth.
 
-The other travels away at a large fraction of the speed of light, turns around, and comes home.
-
-When they reunite, the traveler has accumulated less aging.
-
-Special relativity predicts this.
-
-The clocks can be brought together.
-
-The bodies can stand next to each other.
-
-No remote telescope is needed at the end.
-
-The physical histories really differ.
-
-The apparent paradox comes from a different fact.
-
-During a constant-speed leg, motion is reciprocal.
-
-The Earth twin can say:
-
-> the traveler is moving.
-
-The traveler can say:
-
-> Earth is moving.
-
-So if moving clocks are compared as slower, why does the argument not work equally well in both directions?
-
-And worse:
-
-> the traveler has an outbound leg and an inbound leg. Why not say “Earth ran slower” on both legs, add those readings, and predict that Earth should be younger?
-
-That is the question this Bit has to answer.
